---
# Required.
# id    : becomes the data filename and must be unique across every test.
#         Letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore only.
# title : what shows on the test card.
id: my-test-id
title: My Test Title

# Optional.
# objective   : exam objective or topic line, shown under the title.
# description : one sentence on the card.
# source      : where the material came from (not shown on the site).
# time        : minutes for the "Recommended" timer. Omit or 0 for no default.
# pass        : pass mark as a percentage. Omit or 0 to score without a verdict.
objective: CompTIA Security+ SY0-701, Objective 1.2
description: A short sentence describing what this set covers.
source: Chapter 4 of the course notes
time: 30
pass: 80
---

## Section name

A level-two heading starts a new section. Sections are optional, but with two
or more the results page shows a per-section breakdown, which is what turns a
score into a study plan. Any prose outside a question is ignored by the build.

### Q1
The question stem goes here, on the lines directly under the heading.
It may run across several lines, and may use **bold**, *italic* and `code`.

- A. First option
- B. Second option
- C. Third option
- D. Fourth option

Answer: B
Explain: Optional. Everything after "Explain:" up to the next question is kept,
so an explanation can run to several paragraphs. Say why the right answer is
right AND why the most tempting wrong option is wrong, because that is where
the actual learning is. Leave the whole line out if there is nothing to add;
the review screen then just shows the correct answer.

### Q2
Options do not have to be A to D. Two or more is enough, and the letters only
have to be unique within the question.

- A. True
- B. False

Answer: A

## Another section

### Q3
The heading text after "###" is just a label shown during review, so "Q3",
"Question 3" or "Firewalls" all work. Numbering on the site comes from the
order questions appear in the file, not from the label.

- A. Wrong
- B. Right
- C. Also wrong

Answer: B
Explain: Keep explanations free of lines that begin with "- A." or "Answer:",
since the compiler reads those as structure rather than prose.

---

## Splitting a large set across files

Instead of one big file, a test may be a folder containing `test.md` plus any
number of sibling `.md` files, merged in filename order:

    tests/secplus/my-test/
        test.md        frontmatter, and optionally the first questions
        part-02.md     questions only
        part-03.md     questions only

Only `test.md` carries frontmatter. Sections carry across files, and questions
are numbered by final merged order. Use this when adding questions to an
existing set, so the new ones go in a new file rather than forcing a rewrite of
a large one.

## Rules the build enforces

A push fails, and nothing is published, if any of these is true:

1. The `Answer:` letter is not one of that question's options. This is the
   dangerous one: a typo here silently teaches the wrong fact.
2. A question has fewer than two options, an empty option, or no stem.
3. A question has no `Answer:` line, or more than one.
4. A question lists the same option letter twice.
5. Two tests share an `id`.
6. The frontmatter is missing, unclosed, or has no `id` or `title`.
7. A part file of a split test carries frontmatter.
8. More than 40% of the answers in a set of twelve or more questions land on
   the same letter, or a letter is never correct across twenty or more
   questions. Either way a candidate could score by guessing position instead
   of knowing the material.

Check your work before pushing with:

    python3 tools/build.py --strict
